                        "sense_id": "425c38ff-5bec-3d05-addd-86f1312713e6",
                        "entry_id": "cadaf707-339c-3312-a04a-8520f7caa6d4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Jangling."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c1e5306a-5b4a-4e3f-9170-94d1a80fccfc",
                                "sense_id": "425c38ff-5bec-3d05-addd-86f1312713e6",
                                "text": "1913, Bert Leston Taylor, “Canopus” in Motley Measures, Chicago: The Laurentian Publishers, p. 14,\nWhen men are calling names and making faces,\nAnd all the world’s ajangle and ajar,\nI meditate on interstellar spaces\nAnd smoke a mild seegar."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "27166cdc-e7e4-43af-9064-fa42c52a1be4",
                                "sense_id": "425c38ff-5bec-3d05-addd-86f1312713e6",
                                "text": "Where the horrible clamor had merely set my nerves ajangle, it had plainly lacerated her like some evil bullwhip."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "af8eea6b-5ac0-4c9d-be52-78c59f262d4a",
                                "sense_id": "425c38ff-5bec-3d05-addd-86f1312713e6",
                                "text": "That actually did make Cora feel better—enough that she took a deep breath and reminded herself that the mission was done and successful. No injuries, no pursuit... no reason to still feel so twitchy, her nerves all a-jangle."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
